{
  "unit": {
    "id": "es-a1-u11",
    "title": "Adjectives",
    "subtitle": "Describing things: agreement, colors, and position",
    "level": "A1",
    "order": 11,
    "xp_reward": 20
  },
  "grammar": {
    "title": "Make your adjectives match",
    "intro": "In Spanish an adjective must <b>agree</b> with the noun it describes — in <b>gender</b> (masculine/feminine) and <b>number</b> (singular/plural). And unlike English, the adjective usually comes <b>after</b> the noun: <b>una casa blanca</b> (a white house), not “una blanca casa”.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "tag": "concordancia",
        "heading": "Agreement: the four forms of -o adjectives",
        "body": "An adjective ending in <b>-o</b> has four forms. Change the ending to match the noun:",
        "conj": [
          [
            "masc. singular",
            "alto"
          ],
          [
            "fem. singular",
            "alta"
          ],
          [
            "masc. plural",
            "altos"
          ],
          [
            "fem. plural",
            "altas"
          ]
        ],
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "El chico es <b>alto</b>.",
            "en": "The boy is tall.",
            "note": "masc. sing."
          },
          {
            "es": "Las chicas son <b>altas</b>.",
            "en": "The girls are tall.",
            "note": "fem. plural"
          },
          {
            "es": "Un coche <b>rojo</b>.",
            "en": "A red car.",
            "note": "coche is masculine"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "tag": "otras",
        "heading": "Adjectives ending in -e or a consonant",
        "body": "Adjectives ending in <b>-e</b> (grande, verde) don’t change for gender — only add <b>-s</b> for plural. Adjectives ending in a consonant (azul, gris) add <b>-es</b> for plural:",
        "conj": [
          [
            "grande → plural",
            "grandes"
          ],
          [
            "verde → plural",
            "verdes"
          ],
          [
            "azul → plural",
            "azules"
          ],
          [
            "gris → plural",
            "grises"
          ]
        ],
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "Una casa <b>grande</b> y dos casas <b>grandes</b>.",
            "en": "A big house and two big houses.",
            "note": "-e: same for gender"
          },
          {
            "es": "Los ojos <b>azules</b>.",
            "en": "The blue eyes.",
            "note": "azul → azules"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "tag": "colores",
        "heading": "Colors + position",
        "body": "Colors are adjectives and go <b>after</b> the noun. Common ones: <b>rojo</b> (red), <b>azul</b> (blue), <b>verde</b> (green), <b>blanco</b> (white), <b>negro</b> (black), <b>amarillo</b> (yellow). Use <b>ser</b> to describe: <b>La casa es blanca</b>.",
        "ex": [
          {
            "es": "Una flor <b>amarilla</b>.",
            "en": "A yellow flower.",
            "note": "flor is fem. → amarilla"
          },
          {
            "es": "El libro es <b>negro</b>.",
            "en": "The book is black.",
            "note": "ser + color"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "takeaways": [
      "Adjectives agree with the noun in gender AND number.",
      "-o adjectives have 4 forms: alto / alta / altos / altas.",
      "-e adjectives (grande) add -s; consonant adjectives (azul) add -es for plural.",
      "Descriptive adjectives and colors come after the noun: una casa blanca."
    ],
    "mistakes": [
      {
        "w": "una casa blanco",
        "r": "una casa blanca",
        "why": "Casa is feminine, so the color must be feminine: blanca."
      },
      {
        "w": "los libros rojo",
        "r": "los libros rojos",
        "why": "Libros is plural, so the adjective must be plural: rojos."
      }
    ]
  },
